\section{The shared grade vocabulary, \texttt{gradealiases}}%
\label{gradealiases}

Canvas, and the courses using it, spell the same two outcomes in several
ways.
A passed attempt arrives as [[P]], as \enquote{complete} from Canvas's
built-in pass/fail grading type, or as the course-defined [[P+]].
A failed one arrives as [[F]], as \enquote{incomplete}, as the Canvas label
[[must fix]], or as no grade at all.
Every module that reasons about grades has to know this vocabulary before it
can apply its own policy.

Until now each of them knew it separately.
The grade filters in \cref{p-plus-grades} spelled it as two regular
expressions, [[conjunctavg]] as a chain of [[elif]] branches, [[disjunctmax]]
as the keys of a lookup table.
Three copies of one fact is not merely redundant, it is three chances to be
incomplete---and all three were.
The grade [[P+]] was missing from every one of them, so a single student with
a single [[P+]] hit three different failures depending on which command
they were in: a row silently absent from the export, a [[ValueError]], and a
[[KeyError]] (\cref{p-plus-grades} tells that story).
Fixing them one by one fixes today's alias.
Putting the vocabulary in one module is what makes tomorrow's alias---the next
label Canvas invents, the next grade a course defines---a one-line change in
one place.

\subsection{Vocabulary is shared, policy is not}

The tempting next step is to move everything grade-related here, and it is the
wrong one.
What the modules genuinely agree on is narrow: \emph{which spellings mean a
pass and which mean a failure}.
What they disagree on, deliberately, is what to do about it.
Whether a pass is worth ranking above another pass is [[disjunctmax]]'s
business; whether a missing grade counts as a failure at all depends on
whether the assignment was mandatory.
This module therefore holds two layers, and modules take only the layer they
agree with: the alias tables and the translation built on them, then one
common policy for missing grades that most---but not all---modules share.
