<<module doc>>=
Shared vocabulary of Canvas grade aliases for the summary modules.

Canvas spells the same outcome in several ways: a passed attempt can be
"P", "complete" or the course-defined "P+"; a failed one can be "F",
"incomplete" or "must fix". This module translates those spellings into
the "P" and "F" that the summary modules reason about.

This is a helper module, not a grading strategy: it has no
summarize_group function and cannot be used with `canvaslms results -S`.
@

\subsection{The alias tables}

We keep the two directions in two sets, casefolded, so that a reader can see
the entire vocabulary at a glance and add to it without touching any code.
The sets are exported deliberately: a module whose policy differs from
[[normalize_grade]] below can still build on the vocabulary rather than
inventing its own copy, which is the whole point of this module.

The failing spellings come from two different places.
Canvas's built-in pass/fail grading type reports \enquote{incomplete} for a
failed attempt, and Canvas uses the label [[must fix]] where a teacher asks
for a resubmission.
Neither carries information beyond \enquote{this attempt has not passed}.
<<alias tables>>=
FAILING_ALIASES = {"must fix", "incomplete"}
@

The passing spellings likewise come from two places.
\enquote{complete} is the passing half of Canvas's pass/fail type.
[[P+]] is not a Canvas grade at all: it is a course-defined grade meaning
\enquote{passed, and then some}, which canvaslms already treats as final in
its submission cache (the [[NOREFRESH_GRADES]] policy) and which
[[tilkryLAB1]] awards.
Both mean a pass, and nothing in the P/F scale can express \enquote{and then
some}, so both become [[P]].
<<alias tables>>=
PASSING_ALIASES = {"p+", "complete"}
@

\subsection{Translating an alias}

The translation is the vocabulary layer: it maps the aliases and leaves
everything else exactly as it found it.
Leaving unknown grades untouched is the important half of that contract.
A numeric survey score or a grade from a scale this module knows nothing about
is not a synonym for failure---it is a sign that the assignment's grading
scale and the summary module disagree.
Silently calling it [[F]] would turn that disagreement into a wrong grade for
a real student, so we hand it back unchanged and let the caller, which knows
its own grading scale, decide whether to rank it, warn about it or refuse it.
That is exactly what [[disjunctmax]] does in \cref{disjunctmax}.

We compare casefolded because Canvas writes its own labels in lower case while
course-defined grades are written the way the teacher typed them.
Non-string grades pass straight through: [[casefold]] is only defined for
strings, and a numeric grade is by definition not one of these labels.
<<functions>>=
def normalize_alias(grade):
  """Translate a Canvas grade alias into P or F.

  Grades that are not aliases -- including None, numbers and grades
  from other scales -- are returned unchanged.
  """
  if isinstance(grade, str):
    alias = grade.casefold()
    if alias in FAILING_ALIASES:
      return "F"
    if alias in PASSING_ALIASES:
      return "P"
  return grade
@

Let's verify both directions of the table, the case-insensitivity, and that
everything else is left alone.
<<test functions>>=
def test_failing_aliases_become_f():
  for grade in ["must fix", "Must Fix", "incomplete", "INCOMPLETE"]:
    assert normalize_alias(grade) == "F"


def test_passing_aliases_become_p():
  for grade in ["p+", "P+", "complete", "Complete"]:
    assert normalize_alias(grade) == "P"


def test_ordinary_grades_pass_through():
  for grade in ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F", "Fx", "P"]:
    assert normalize_alias(grade) == grade


def test_unknown_grades_pass_through_untouched():
  for grade in [None, "17.0", 17.0, "", "EP"]:
    assert normalize_alias(grade) == grade
@

\subsection{When there is no grade at all}

A missing grade ([[None]]) is where the modules start to part ways, so it gets
its own function rather than a place in the tables.

For a mandatory letter-graded assignment, no grade means not passed, and both
[[conjunctavg]] and [[disjunctmax]] want to say so.
But that is a policy about mandatory assignments, not a fact about Canvas's
vocabulary, and it is wrong elsewhere.
In [[tilkryLAB1]] an ungraded \emph{optional} assignment is skipped, while an
[[F]] on one contributes zero points---so collapsing [[None]] to [[F]] there
would silently award a zero the teacher never gave.
In [[conjunctavgsurvey]] an ungraded survey is not a failure either: whether
it passed is decided by completion, and that branch never runs if [[None]]
has already become [[F]].

Splitting the two lets each module take what it agrees with.
Those that share the mandatory-assignment policy import [[normalize_grade]];
those that don't can still use [[normalize_alias]] and keep their own
treatment of [[None]].
<<functions>>=
def normalize_grade(grade):
  """Normalize a Canvas grade for mandatory-assignment grading.

  Aliases are translated as by normalize_alias, and a missing grade
  (None) counts as F.
  """
  if grade is None:
    return "F"
  return normalize_alias(grade)
@

The two functions must agree everywhere except on the one grade they were
split over.
<<test functions>>=
def test_missing_grade_counts_as_failing():
  assert normalize_grade(None) == "F"
  assert normalize_alias(None) is None


def test_normalize_grade_translates_the_same_aliases():
  for grade in ["must fix", "incomplete", "p+", "P+", "complete", "A", "17.0"]:
    assert normalize_grade(grade) == normalize_alias(grade)
@

\subsection{Who shares this vocabulary}

Two modules use [[normalize_grade]] as it stands: [[conjunctavg]]
(\cref{conjunctavg}), from which [[conjunctavgsurvey]] imports it in turn, and
[[disjunctmax]] (\cref{disjunctmax}).
Both grade mandatory assignments on a P/F or A--F scale, which is precisely
the policy the missing-grade rule above encodes.

The remaining summary modules deliberately do \emph{not} adopt it, and it is
worth recording why, because \enquote{they forgot} and \enquote{they differ}
look identical from a distance:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[maxgradesurvey]] ] must not treat \enquote{complete} as a pass.
  Surveys in its assignment groups auto-record completion, and it grades
  disjunctively, so a complete-counts-as-P rule would pass a student on
  survey participation alone.
\item[ [[participation]] ] asks whether a grade is \enquote{complete} or
  [[100]] as its passing test.
  Normalizing \enquote{complete} to [[P]] before that test would make every
  passing assignment fail it, so adopting the vocabulary here means rewriting
  the test at the same time.
\item[ [[tilkryLAB1]] ] must keep [[None]] distinct, as described above.
\end{description}
Each of them could still use [[normalize_alias]] and the tables; none of them
should be changed to use [[normalize_grade]] without changing the policy that
follows it.

One consumer stays outside this module altogether: the grade filters of
\cref{p-plus-grades}.
They match the raw text Canvas returned, before anything here has run, and the
pattern is user-supplied through [[-F]]---so they must describe Canvas's
spelling rather than our normalized tokens, and they cannot be generated from
the tables above.
That is the one place where adding an alias means editing two files, and it is
recorded here so the second one isn't forgotten.
